WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:06.630
I want to take a second and sort of step back
from the micro of the virus and think a little

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:11.720
bit about the macro, because this is not getting
nearly the attention that it deserves.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:18.710
One of the things that the virus has exposed
is just how fragile and precarious, not just

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:25.300
the American economy is, but how fragile and
precarious the entire planet's economy and

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.460
circumstances are.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:30.760
And this is not an attack on any particular
politician.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:34.830
There are many critiques to make of particular
politicians, including our own here in the

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:35.830
United.

00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:39.630
Gates But this is actually a bigger picture
issue in sort of a realization that we have

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:40.750
to come to.

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:45.300
Yesterday, Donald Trump announced the U.S.
is invoking the Defense Production Act and

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:52.050
that we would be dipping into our stockpile
of masks and ventilators because the medical

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.600
system is so quickly becoming overwhelmed.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.590
And remember, we're early in this thing.

00:00:56.590 --> 00:00:58.920
We're still very early in this thing.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:04.640
And it's important to know that that stockpile
is really met for a truly massive crisis that

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:09.920
is out of control in the models, something
way more serious than this virus.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.240
Now, I want to be careful.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:13.829
I'm not minimizing this virus.

00:01:13.829 --> 00:01:15.100
This is a crisis.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:16.890
We're doing what we can to deal with it.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:18.320
We've been covering it as a crisis.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:21.689
We've got a social distance, self isolate,
all of that stuff.

00:01:21.689 --> 00:01:26.560
But I also want to make sure that we understand
that this is a stockpile that needs to be

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:33.570
there in case of something like without being
dark, a nuclear attack, a flu pandemic with

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:35.700
a really high death rate like think.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:39.119
Fifteen percent, a very serious death rate
flu.

00:01:39.119 --> 00:01:44.969
The fact that we're just in the first week
of when Trump finally stopped saying this

00:01:44.969 --> 00:01:50.179
is no big deal and is being serious about
it and we're already dipping into the stockpile

00:01:50.179 --> 00:01:53.149
that's meant to be there for those super crises.

00:01:53.149 --> 00:01:58.249
This is a virus that, again, in the end is
likely to have a death rate under 1 percent.

00:01:58.249 --> 00:02:01.240
Doesn't minimize the number of people that
may die.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:02.469
I want to be really clear.

00:02:02.469 --> 00:02:06.899
But what I'm explaining is once you account
for the real number of cases here, which could

00:02:06.899 --> 00:02:10.880
be orders of magnitude greater, is likely
orders of magnitude greater than than the

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:12.050
official numbers.

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:16.030
The death rate may end up somewhere between
point seven and 1 percent.

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:21.170
And we're dipping into stockpiles that are
meant to be there for something far more serious.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:26.530
This has triggered a likely global recession
and the stock market has fallen now below

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:28.980
the level where it was when Trump took office.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:33.190
So we've seen a net stock market loss under
Donald Trump.

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:35.010
People are losing four one KS.

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:37.160
Thousands of people are dying.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:41.280
We're talking about massive unemployment increases,
trillions of dollars in emergency government

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:42.480
spending.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:47.700
And so to be really clear, this isn't about
minimizing the seriousness of this virus.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:52.590
And in fact, we've been from day one making
sure we don't minimize it and that people

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:55.260
know it's serious and that we have to act
now.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:03:00.250
But what I want you to consider is that if
things are this out of control now, we're

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:03.640
we're already dipping into these stockpiles.

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:07.980
If it's serious enough that I'm certainly
going to remember this period for the rest

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:10.420
of my life, assuming I make it out.

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:16.130
Just imagine what would be going on if this
was a more deadly flu, a nuclear attack or

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:17.980
some other major event.

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:23.710
We are incredibly unprepared and having an
incompetent president doesn't help.

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:25.690
But this is bigger than Donald Trump.

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:30.460
Even when we think about the bailouts that
are now correctly going to have to be done,

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:36.560
a lot of it relates to the structural liabilities
and the precarious nature of our economy.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:41.400
This whole discussion about restaurant workers
who need to be bailed out, it's an accurate

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:42.400
discussion.

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:43.840
They need to be bailed out.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:48.730
But if we had a better infrastructure for
paid leave for such restaurant workers, this

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:50.990
wouldn't be such an immediate crisis.

00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:55.090
The declarations that Donald Trump and governors
had to make about we've got to change how

00:03:55.090 --> 00:04:00.540
health insurance deals with the cost of testing
and treatment because this is an emergency.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:06.400
All of that would be made irrelevant if our
health care system was more stable and sensible

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:08.040
from day one.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.490
I said it yesterday and I'll say it again.

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:12.990
An ounce of prevention is worth a pound of
cure.

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:16.889
And it couldn't possibly be more clear than
with this current situation.

00:04:16.889 --> 00:04:22.870
And the reality that we all have to understand
is this is a feature of our economic system,

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:23.870
not a bug.

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:26.389
It feels like a bug because now we're in crisis.

00:04:26.389 --> 00:04:32.009
But this is a feature redundancy and being
proactive costs money.

00:04:32.009 --> 00:04:33.789
If you just react.

00:04:33.789 --> 00:04:37.659
It only costs you money when the you know
what hits the fan.

00:04:37.659 --> 00:04:40.900
Warehouses, surplus staff, et cetera.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:45.629
When the market plays too big of a role, this
is what happens.

00:04:45.629 --> 00:04:47.530
That stuff is just too expensive.

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:52.819
When the private sector has too much power,
this is what happens now.

00:04:52.819 --> 00:04:59.159
Although this isn't meant to blame Trump,
the entire world is to some degree underprepared.

00:04:59.159 --> 00:05:03.039
We also do need to recognize that there is
blame here to assign.

00:05:03.039 --> 00:05:07.139
And in the United States, one of our two major
political parties, the Republican Party, is

00:05:07.139 --> 00:05:13.259
the one that believes readiness, preparedness,
that it's wasteful spending that also has

00:05:13.259 --> 00:05:16.580
to change for good, because otherwise we're
not going to learn anything from this or at

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:20.960
least nothing that we will actually be able
to get consensus on and act on.

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:26.129
I don't know whether this fiasco will change
anything for the Republican Party.

00:05:26.129 --> 00:05:30.189
They tend not to learn from mistakes, but
they should.

00:05:30.189 --> 00:05:34.680
And then lastly, the other scary reality is
that the virus has.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:40.689
Shows how unprepared the U.S. is for any kind
of biological attack as well.

00:05:40.689 --> 00:05:45.719
And I know that, you know, we don't want to
add more onto what we're dealing with now.

00:05:45.719 --> 00:05:48.409
A lot of us don't want to be thinking about
that.

00:05:48.409 --> 00:05:50.069
But that is the truth.

00:05:50.069 --> 00:05:58.009
And the goal would be you learn from mistakes
and then you become more resilient.

00:05:58.009 --> 00:06:04.559
Nick Naseem, Nicholas Taleb writes about A.I
fragility in systems and at the individual

00:06:04.559 --> 00:06:08.860
level, and he writes about black swan events,
two things that are very, very relevant.

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:12.529
Right now, I encourage you to check out his
books, which are very good.

00:06:12.529 --> 00:06:15.650
It's not often what people want to think about.

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:17.340
But we need to be thinking about it.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:19.410
And this has really exposed a lot.

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:27.169
And lastly, later in the program, this entire
crisis has significantly improved the climate

00:06:27.169 --> 00:06:28.650
change situation.

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:32.210
And that is something I hope we will actually
learn from.

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:34.120
I'll deal with that a little bit later.

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:35.120
Let me know your thoughts.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:40.499
We'll be posting about this on the David Packman
show Instagram page at David Packman Show.

00:06:40.499 --> 00:06:44.749
Follow that and also follow me on Instagram
at David Dot Packman.

